-- title:
--   Multivalued variables, the indicator algebra A(X), block degree, indicator designs and pseudoexpectations, booleanization D^bool (Definitions 2.9–2.15)
-- statement:
--   A collection of **multivalued variables** is a finite set $V$ of variables $x_v$ with finite ranges $S_v=\{a_{v,0},\dots,a_{v,|S_v|-1}\}$. An **indicator** $[\![x_v=j]\!]$ is $1$ iff $x_v=a_{v,j}$. An **indicator conjunction** is a product of indicators, and a **multivalued clause** is a disjunction $\bigvee_i[\![x_{v_i}\neq j_i]\!]$. The **block degree** $\mathrm{bdeg}(C)$ of an indicator conjunction is the number of distinct variables it mentions (Definition 2.9).
--
--   The **indicator algebra** (Definition 2.11) is $A(X)=\mathbb R[I(X)]/\mathcal J$, where $\mathcal J$ is generated by $1-\sum_{i}[\![x_v=i]\!]$, $[\![x_v=i]\!]^2-[\![x_v=i]\!]$ and $[\![x_v=i]\!][\![x_v=k]\!]$ for $i\neq k$. For a partial assignment $\sigma$ (each variable set to a value or left unset), $\chi_\sigma=\prod_{\sigma(x_v)\neq *}[\![x_v=\sigma(x_v)]\!]$ (Definition 2.10), and $A(X)_{\le d}=\mathrm{span}\{\chi_\sigma:|\sigma|\le d\}$ (Definition 2.12).
--
--   A **degree-$d$ indicator design** (Definition 2.13) is a map $D$ on $A(X)_{\le d}$ with $D[1]=1$ that is linear. It is **$F$-nonwitnessing** for a multivalued CNF $F$ if $D[C]=0$ for every clause $C_i\in F$ and every indicator conjunction $C\Rightarrow\bar C_i$ with $\mathrm{bdeg}(C)\le d$. An **indicator pseudoexpectation** (Definition 2.14) also has $D[C]\ge0$ for every indicator conjunction with $\mathrm{bdeg}(C)\le d$.
--
--   **Booleanization** (p. 21, Definition 2.15). When $|S_v|=2^{w_v}$, the variable $x_v$ is encoded by bits $x_{v,0},\dots,x_{v,w_v-1}$ with $x_v=a_{v,j}$ iff $\sum_k2^kx_{v,k}=j$. Then $[\![x_v=j]\!]^{\mathrm{bool}}$ is the conjunction of the bit literals $x_{v,k}=\mathrm{bit}_k(j)$, $C^{\mathrm{bool}}=\prod_iI_i^{\mathrm{bool}}$, a multivalued clause becomes the disjunction of the negated bit literals, and $F^{\mathrm{bool}}$ is the resulting CNF. For a boolean conjunction $C$ over the bits, let $I_C$ be the set of multivalued variables it mentions. Then
--   $$D^{\mathrm{bool}}[C]=\sum_{\sigma:\ \mathrm{dom}\,\sigma=I_C,\ \sigma(C)=1}D[\chi_\sigma],$$
--   extended linearly to $\mathbb R[x,\bar x]$, a power $x_b^e$ or $\bar x_b^e$ ($e\ge1$) of a monomial being read as $x_b$ or $\bar x_b$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The range $S_v$ is `Fin (size v)` (value $a_{v,j}\leftrightarrow j$), an indicator is a pair `⟨v, j⟩`, and indicator conjunctions and multivalued clauses are finite sets of indicators. Designs are functions on all of $A(X)$ of which only the values on $A(X)_{\le d}$ are constrained. `evalAlg a` evaluates $A(X)$ at a total assignment; it is well defined because it kills every generator of $\mathcal J$. Reading powers as their base is the reduction modulo the boolean and complementarity ideal that the paper uses to extend $D^{\mathrm{bool}}$ beyond multilinear polynomials.
-- source:
--   Acheampong, Davis, Robere, Polynomial Interpolation for Sherali-Adams Lower Bounds, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 8de0a787…), pp. 19–21, Definitions 2.9–2.15 and the binary encoding before Definition 2.15

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MultiplierAssoc206_Resolution_CNF
import Definitions.Def_SAInterp_Res2_SherAdams

